{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Give Cloud the resilience and config-validation coverage DC gained\n\n## Context\n\nEpic e369 hardened the DC adapter against a real instance and produced a tier of coverage Cloud\nnever had. Cloud's tests grew from INCIDENTS THAT HAPPENED; DC's grew from CONFIGURATIONS THAT\nDIFFER. The second kind generalises, and Cloud does not have it.\n\nThis story is the Cloud half of the parity work. It is lower priority than\n[rebar:0fad-59f7-1605-4377] (Cloud's unpaginated whole-project readers) because Cloud is the mature,\nlive-exercised path — but each item below is a real asymmetry, not a symmetry exercise.\n\n## The gaps, with what DC has for comparison\n\n**1. No per-member 429 sweep.** DC's `test_dc_rate_limit_retry_heldout.py` walks EVERY mutating\ntransport member and asserts each fails on the first 429 — because retrying a mutation on an HTTP\nerror is the duplicate-issue class [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] fixed, and an enumeration is what\nstops a later-added mutation inheriting a retry by omission. Cloud has\n`test_retry_httperror.py` (5 tests) and one 429 path in\n`state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:352-368`, neither of which sweeps the member set.\n\n**DC's property does NOT transfer uniformly, and assuming it does is the main risk in this item.**\nCloud has no single choke point; its mutations traverse FOUR distinct 429 behaviours across two\nlevels, all verified in the tree. Two are transport-level and DELIBERATELY OPPOSITE:\n\n* **direct REST** (`AcliRestMixin._rest_urlopen_with_retry`) — \"Does NOT retry on\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError` (4xx / 5xx)\" (`adapters/jira/acli_rest.py:50-52`). A 429 arrives as\n  `urllib.error.HTTPError`. Here DC's property holds verbatim: exactly ONE attempt.\n* **ACLI subprocess** (`acli_subprocess._run_acli`) — **already RETRIES a 429**, on purpose, via\n  `_rate_limit_backoff` honouring `Retry-After`; `test_run_acli_429_retries_with_rate_limit_backoff`\n  (`state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:369`) asserts the call SUCCEEDS on retry. A 429 here is an\n  exit code plus `429` in stderr, not an HTTP exception.\n\n…and ABOVE both sits the reconciler's dispatch level, which is the part most likely to be missed —\nand it is itself SPLIT IN TWO:\n\n* **the retrying dispatch wrapper** — `dispatch_one._call_with_retry`\n  (`dispatch_one.py:113-186`) catches `urllib.error.HTTPError` and, when `exc.code == 429`, records\n  `Retry-After` and RETRIES (`dispatch_one.py:151-160`). So a REST mutation that makes exactly one\n  attempt when its transport method is called DIRECTLY is nevertheless retried when reached the way\n  production reaches it.\n* **the explicitly NON-retrying apply phases** — `dispatch_apply_phases`, whose module docstring\n  states it owns \"the phases that do NOT funnel their Jira writes through\n  `dispatch_one._call_with_retry`\". These include real mutations:\n  `_update_one_apply_reporter` (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:91`) and\n  `_update_one_dispatch_comments` (`:142`, the single-attempt comment dispatch). A 429 here is a\n  FOURTH behaviour — neither the subprocess backoff nor the dispatch-wrapper retry applies — and\n  existing coverage (`test_comment_single_attempt.py`) pins attempt count for ONE member, not a\n  sweep.\n\nSo \"every mutating member fails on the first 429\" is TRUE for a raw REST transport method called\ndirectly, TRUE again for the non-retrying `dispatch_apply_phases` mutations, and FALSE for both the\nsubprocess seam and anything routed through the retrying dispatch wrapper. **An attempt-count\nassertion is therefore meaningless unless it names the BOUNDARY it measures at.** Writing the DC assertion across all four would either fail immediately\nor pressure someone into deleting an intentional, tested backoff. This story must establish the\nsplit FIRST and assert a per-layer property — and it must not fold in a behaviour change (below).\n\n**The two retries named above are KNOWN and DELIBERATE — they are not the bug trigger.** Both\n`_run_acli`'s rate-limit backoff and `_call_with_retry`'s 429 branch are already implemented,\ntested and documented, so \"a mutation is retried on a 429\" is not by itself a finding; the\nenumeration will hit them by construction. The bug-filing trigger is NARROWER: a mutating member\nretried on a 429 at a layer where that retry is NOT already explicitly tested and documented — an\nUNDOCUMENTED or newly-discovered retry path. That is the [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] duplicate\nclass. If one is found, file a linked bug with the evidence and do NOT \"fix\" it inside this test\nstory. Separately, if the enumeration shows a KNOWN retry applying to a mutation in a way nobody has\nevaluated for duplicate-write safety, record that assessment on this ticket — as a finding, not as a\ncode change.\n\n**2. No config-validation tier.** DC asserts a non-HTTPS base URL is rejected, that the insecure\noverride warns, that malformed config propagates, and that a missing credential fails loudly rather\nthan going anonymous (`test_jira_dc_config_settings.py`, `test_dc_pat_required_heldout.py` — the\nlatter exists because bug cd78 was exactly a silent fall-back to ANONYMOUS access). Cloud has no\nequivalent. Whether Cloud CAN go anonymous is itself unestablished and worth answering.\n\n**3. The probe's resolved-status set is not runtime-configurable.** `adapters/jira/probe.py:39` pins\n`frozenset({\"Resolved\",\"Done\",\"Cancelled\"})`; DC threads `resolved_statuses` from settings and\ntests it. Cloud's `probe_remote` also re-resolves env itself rather than using the transport's\nconfigured session.\n\n**\"Is it deliberate?\" is already answered — do NOT re-open it.** `probe.py`'s module docstring\n(`:20-25`) states that J2 moved the pure classifier to `adapters/jira_family/probe.py` taking the\nstatus set as a PARAMETER, and that `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES` \"is Cloud/DIG's *configured value* (a\nself-hosted Data Center workflow can name its resolved states anything), so it stays defined here\".\nSo it is documented as deliberate at the definition site, and an AC satisfied by \"document it\" would\nbe satisfied by doing nothing. The remaining OPEN question is narrower and is the only one this item\nanswers: **does a Cloud deployment whose workflow uses non-standard resolved-status names need a\nruntime config hook** (as DC has), or is the per-deployment constant genuinely sufficient? Record\nTHAT decision with its rationale; if a hook is needed, file it as its own ticket.\n\n**4. `search_user_by_email` degradation is unasserted on DC** (the mirror-image item, kept here so\nthe pair is not split). `outbound_assignee.py:33-37` resolves the user-search method by NAME STRING\nacross three aliases; DC provides none of them, so `_bootstrap_account_id_via_user_search` returns\n`None` and the path degrades to string matching. That degradation is probably correct — DC has no\naccountId concept — but nothing asserts it, so an accidental rename on the Cloud side would look\nlike the same silence.\n\n## Approach\n\nTake these in the order above; 1 and 2 carry the risk, 3 is an investigation that may produce a\nticket rather than a test, 4 is a one-test pin.\n\n**Port the PROPERTY, not the test — and note the property is PER-LAYER.** Cloud's retry lives in a\ndifferent place from DC's, so a copied test would assert the wrong seam. What must survive the port\nis the ENUMERATION — that every mutating member is named explicitly, because an ellipsis is how the\nnext mutation inherits a retry.\n\n**Step 0 for item 1, before any test is written:** enumerate every mutating member of Cloud's\ntransport and classify each on BOTH axes, by reading bodies rather than inferring from names:\n\n1. **transport seam** — one of THREE: ACLI subprocess (`_run_acli`); pooled direct REST\n   (`_rest_urlopen_with_retry` / `_direct_rest_post_raw` / `_direct_rest_put_raw`); or **bare\n   `urllib.request.urlopen`**, which bypasses `_rest_urlopen_with_retry` entirely and therefore does\n   not even inherit its transient-fault retry — `acli_cli_ops.update_priority`\n   (`acli_cli_ops.py:396`, urlopen at `:437`) is a confirmed instance, reached in production because\n   `AcliClient.update_issue` delegates to the free function `update_issue` (`acli.py:374`), which\n   calls `update_priority` when `priority` is among the changed fields (`acli.py:180`). A member with\n   more than one leg (e.g. a REST leg and a subprocess leg) is called out as such.\n2. **dispatch level** — which of THREE the reconciler uses: the retrying wrapper\n   `dispatch_one._call_with_retry` (`dispatch_one.py:113-186`), which adds a 429 retry ON TOP of\n   whatever the seam does; the explicitly non-retrying apply phases in `dispatch_apply_phases`\n   (`_update_one_apply_reporter:91`, `_update_one_dispatch_comments:142`); or no dispatch wrapper at\n   all. Determine this by reading the call site, since the module split is by RETRY POLICY, so the\n   answer is not inferable from the member's own body.\n\n**Completeness check for the table, run and recorded alongside it** — the table gates the whole\nitem, so its coverage must be demonstrated, not asserted. Enumerate the candidate surface with a\nconcrete command and account for EVERY name it returns:\n\n    git grep -nE \"^(    )?def [a-z]\" -- \\\n      src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py \\\n      src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_graph.py \\\n      src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_rest.py \\\n      src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py\n\nThe pattern must match BOTH indented class methods and MODULE-LEVEL free functions, and\n`acli_cli_ops.py` must be in the file list: `AcliClient` delegates real mutations to free functions\nthere (`create_issue:147`, `update_priority:396`, `add_comment:469`), so a class-methods-only scan\nreports a table that looks complete while omitting the members that actually issue the writes.\n\nPaste that raw output on the ticket next to the table. Every name in it is either classified in the\ntable or explicitly marked NON-MUTATING with a one-line reason — no name may be silently absent.\nCross-check the mutating set against DC's enumerated list in `test_dc_rate_limit_retry_heldout.py`\nand record any member DC sweeps that Cloud's table lacks, or vice versa, with the reason.\n\nThe table decides, per member, which property is asserted and at which boundary it is observed — and\na member can legitimately have DIFFERENT correct answers at the two levels (one attempt raw, retried\nwhen dispatch-wrapped).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — Cloud 429 sweep, Cloud config-validation tests, a DC\n  user-search-degradation pin.\n- an investigation note (or a linked bug) on the Cloud probe's hardcoded resolved statuses.\n- the per-member seam classification table for item 1 (recorded on the ticket, not in code).\n- No production change intended. Items 1 and 3 may each produce a linked BUG — item 1 only if a\n  mutating member is retried on a 429 at a layer where that retry is NOT already tested and\n  documented (the two known deliberate retries do not count), item 3 if the hardcoded\n  resolved-status set is an accident — and in both cases the fix is its own ticket, never folded in\n  here.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe 429 sweep must assert the ATTEMPT COUNT per member, not merely that an exception escaped — an\nimplementation that retried and then re-raised satisfies a raises-only assertion having issued the\nwrite twice. This is the same correction being applied to DC's own sweep in\n[rebar:2944-dd3f-e982-4359]; do not copy that mistake forward.\n\n**The instrument differs per seam, and a single patch point covers only half the members** — a sweep\nthat patches `urllib.request.urlopen` is blind to subprocess mutations, and one that patches\n`acli_subprocess._run_acli` is blind to REST mutations, while either LOOKS complete because every\nmember still raises. So:\n\n* REST-seam members, called DIRECTLY on the transport (not through the dispatch wrapper) — observe\n  attempts at the urlopen boundary; inject a 429 as `urllib.error.HTTPError`; assert EXACTLY ONE\n  attempt (no retry), matching `acli_rest.py:50-52`.\n* the DISPATCH-WRAPPED path — the same members reached through `dispatch_one._call_with_retry`\n  DO retry a 429 by design (`dispatch_one.py:151-160`). Assert the retry is bounded and honours\n  `Retry-After`; do NOT assert one attempt here. This is the layer where \"is a MUTATION being\n  retried on a 429?\" is actually decided, so it is the layer the bug-filing trigger below applies to.\n* subprocess-seam members — observe attempts at the `_run_acli`/`Popen` boundary; inject a 429 as\n  the exit-code + `429`-in-stderr shape the existing test uses; assert the OBSERVED, INTENDED\n  behaviour (a bounded retry honouring `Retry-After`, per\n  `state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py:369`) rather than one attempt — and record whether that\n  retry applies to a MUTATION, which is the bug-filing trigger above.\n\nEach seam's sweep must be MUTATION-CHECKED at its own instrument: make one member issue an extra\nattempt at that boundary and confirm THAT seam's sweep goes red — proving the sweep is not blind to\nthe half it is supposed to cover.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] every MUTATING Cloud transport member is enumerated explicitly — no ellipsis, no sampling —\n      and classified on BOTH axes: transport seam (ACLI subprocess / pooled direct REST / bare\n      `urllib.request.urlopen`) and dispatch\n      level (retrying `dispatch_one._call_with_retry`, the explicitly NON-retrying\n      `dispatch_apply_phases` phases, or no wrapper). The table lands as a REPO ARTIFACT — the sweep\n      test module's own enumeration plus a header comment carrying the per-member classification,\n      which is where a reviewer will look — and is mirrored onto this ticket\n- [ ] the table's COMPLETENESS is demonstrated, not asserted: the enumeration command from Step 0 is\n      run, its raw output recorded beside the table, and every name it returns is either classified\n      or marked NON-MUTATING with a reason — plus the DC cross-check for members present on one side\n      and missing on the other\n- [ ] each REST-seam mutating member, invoked DIRECTLY on the transport, is asserted to make EXACTLY\n      ONE attempt under a 429, observed at the urlopen boundary\n- [ ] the DISPATCH-WRAPPED path is covered separately: reached through\n      `dispatch_one._call_with_retry`, a 429 IS retried by design (`dispatch_one.py:151-160`), so the\n      assertion there is a BOUNDED retry honouring `Retry-After` — not one attempt\n- [ ] each subprocess-seam mutating member is asserted against its ACTUAL intended 429 behaviour —\n      the bounded `Retry-After` backoff in `_run_acli` — observed at the `_run_acli` boundary. This\n      criterion is NOT satisfied by asserting one attempt there, because\n      `test_run_acli_429_retries_with_rate_limit_backoff` proves the retry is deliberate\n- [ ] mutations reached through the NON-retrying `dispatch_apply_phases` path\n      (`_update_one_apply_reporter`, `_update_one_dispatch_comments`) are asserted to make exactly\n      one attempt under a 429 — extending `test_comment_single_attempt.py`'s single-member pin to\n      the sweep\n- [ ] if a MUTATING member is retried on a 429 at a layer where that retry is NOT already tested and\n      documented, a linked bug is filed with the evidence. The two KNOWN deliberate retries\n      (`_run_acli`'s rate-limit backoff, `_call_with_retry`'s 429 branch) do NOT by themselves\n      trigger a bug; where one applies to a mutation, the duplicate-write assessment is RECORDED\n      here instead. This story changes NO retry behaviour either way\n- [ ] Cloud rejects a non-HTTPS base URL, or the test records that it deliberately does not\n- [ ] Cloud's behaviour on a MISSING credential is asserted: it must fail loudly, or the test pins\n      that it does not and a bug is filed (this is bug cd78's shape on the other adapter)\n- [ ] the OPEN question on the Cloud probe's resolved-status set is answered: whether a Cloud\n      deployment using non-standard resolved-status names needs a runtime config hook as DC has, or\n      whether the per-deployment constant suffices. The answer lands as a REPO ARTIFACT, not only a\n      ticket comment — a comment at `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES` in `adapters/jira/probe.py` recording the\n      decision and citing this ticket id (plus the follow-up ticket id if a hook is needed) — so a\n      reviewer can find it without the tracker. Re-documenting it as merely \"deliberate\" does NOT\n      satisfy this: `probe.py:20-25` already says so, and an AC already true on an unmodified tree is\n      not a criterion\n- [ ] a test pins that DC exposes none of `_USER_SEARCH_METHODS` and that the bootstrap degrades to\n      the string-match path rather than raising\n- [ ] each new test is MUTATION-CHECKED. \"Mutation-checked\" here means a CONCRETE MANUAL mutation,\n      not a mutation-testing tool: for each new test, apply the named perturbation below, run that\n      test module, record it RED with its pass/fail counts, revert, record it GREEN again. Both\n      verdicts go in a REPO-VISIBLE place — the commit/Gerrit change description of the change that\n      adds the tests, listing per test the mutation applied and the RED/GREEN pass-fail counts — as\n      well as on this ticket, so the evidence is reviewable from the change itself and survives\n      independently of the tracker. The named mutations are: for a REST-seam sweep, make one member retry\n      once at the urlopen boundary (the `== 1` attempt assertion must go red); for a subprocess-seam\n      sweep, SUPPRESS the retry outright — cap `_run_acli`'s attempt loop at one, or make it re-raise\n      on a 429 exit without looping — so the ATTEMPT-COUNT assertion goes red. Note what does NOT\n      work: making `_rate_limit_backoff` return `None` does not remove the retry at all, because\n      `acli_subprocess.py:429` is\n      `_backoff_sleep(rl_delay if rl_delay is not None else 2 ** (attempt + 1))` — a `None` merely\n      swaps `Retry-After` for a uniform exponential delay and the call is still retried;\n      for the non-HTTPS-URL test, FIRST establish whether Cloud has a scheme rejection at all —\n      `_validate_reconciler_tls` (`_config_schema.py:78`) is invoked only from\n      `ReconcilerConfig.__post_init__` (`:427`), and Cloud's `AcliClient` / settings resolution has\n      no scheme check of its own, so the likely answer is that Cloud does NOT reject `http://`. If it\n      DOES reject, disable that rejection and confirm the test goes RED. If it does NOT, the test is\n      absence-documenting, and its mutation check is the inverse: ADD a scheme assertion to the\n      production path, confirm the absence-documenting test goes RED, then revert — record which of\n      the two cases applied. Do NOT hunt for a non-existent hook, and do NOT add one permanently\n      (a production change, out of scope); for the missing-credential test, make\n      the production path proceed WITHOUT raising when the credential is absent (the cd78\n      anonymous-fallback shape) and confirm the test goes RED; for the `_USER_SEARCH_METHODS`\n      degradation pin, add one of the three alias names to the DC stub and confirm the pin goes RED.\n      Every named perturbation above must be one that makes its test FAIL — a step that merely\n      exercises the passing path is not a mutation check"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785702032007180001,"uuid":"0d80e58e-a758-4b5d-a5c9-69944c5683f9"}